From: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Subject: mm/madvise: notify userfaultfd when installing guard regions
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 19:02:51 -0800

MADV_GUARD_INSTALL can zap populated pages before installing guard
markers, but does not send UFFD_EVENT_REMOVE. Consider an anonymous
mapping registered for userfaultfd MISSING faults whose manager retains
a saved copy of a page. Guard installation discards the page without
telling the manager to discard its copy. After MADV_GUARD_REMOVE, an
access faults on the now-empty mapping; the manager can copy the old
bytes back instead of supplying zeros. The task then reads data it
discarded. If it reuses the range assuming it is empty, stale contents
can enter its calculations or output, and sensitive contents can become
visible to later users of that range. This depends on the manager's
handling of saved pages; it is not a demonstrated kernel memory leak or
privilege escalation.

An unprivileged task can call MADV_GUARD_INSTALL on its own eligible
mapping even when another process manages the userfaultfd. Setting up
the userfaultfd and receiving REMOVE events require an appropriately
registered manager and UFFD_FEATURE_EVENT_REMOVE; unprivileged creation
of that manager's userfaultfd depends on system policy. Guard installation
also discards uffd-wp state. Without MISSING registration to report the
next fault, a manager relying on that protection can miss later writes.
RWP applies to present PTEs, so this patch does not claim a separate
demonstrated RWP data-loss path.

Send UFFD_EVENT_REMOVE before installing the guards, as MADV_DONTNEED
does. If the event drops mmap_lock, reacquire it and look up and validate
the VMA again before modifying it. Limit the current operation to the
revalidated VMA; madvise_walk_vmas() handles any remaining range.

Tal Zussman previously proposed this notification [1]. This version is
based on the tree containing Lorenzo Stoakes's marker-dropping fix [2].

